(Bug #71 - a precision specifier used to be applied before the value's type
 was ever consulted, so `{n:.2}` on a number read the integer's 64 bits as
 an IEEE-754 double and printed `0.00`. 255's bits are 1.26e-321, which
 rounds to 0.00 at two places; every whole number below about 2^62 printed
 `0.00` the same way, which is why the wrong answer never looked like a bit
 pattern.

 LANGUAGE.md:3175 writes the rule of `{var:.N}` with no type attached, and
 the designer's number/float ruling recorded on #65 makes a whole number a
 member of the same family, so a count of decimal places applies to one:
 255 to two places is 255.00.

 The big-number rows are the reason this is rendered as the integer, a
 point and N zeros rather than by converting to a double first. 2^53+1 and
 i64::MAX have no exact double, so a conversion would print
 9007199254740992.00 and 9223372036854775808.0 - rounding a value on its
 way to being printed "exactly, correctly rounded", which is #34's defect.
 Digits-then-zeros is exact for every i64.

 The last row is LANGUAGE.md:3226's promise that a specifier renders the
 same in every sink: the text built from the same hole must read the same
 as the printed one.)
a number called n is 255.
a float called ratio is 2.5.
a boolean called ready is true.

Print "{n:.2}".
Print "{n:.0}".
Print "{n:.5}".
Print "{ratio:.2}".
Print "{ready:.2}".

a number called owed is 0 subtract 5.
Print "{owed:.2}".

a number called nought is 0.
Print "{nought:.3}".

a number called 'no double holds this' is 9007199254740993.
Print "{no double holds this:.2}".

a number called largest is 9223372036854775807.
Print "{largest:.1}".

a text called built is "{n:.2}".
Print "{built}".
